1. Work performed: In [fused_moe_decode.cu](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-inference/runtime/src/backend/fused_moe_decode.cu#L299) I added a new `DeviceExpertSelectionKernel<<<1,32>>>` fast path for the Nano shape. Each lane owns 4 experts, computes `sigmoid(router_logits[e])` plus `correction_bias[e]`, keeps local top-1, then does 6 rounds of warp-shuffle reduction to pick the global winner, mask it, and repeat. Lane 0 writes `selected_indices` and `selected_weights`, then optionally renormalizes and applies `routed_scaling_factor`. The old serial path was kept as `DeviceExpertSelectionLegacyKernel`, and [RunDeviceExpertSelection()](/home/khkramer/src/nemotron-inference/runtime/src/backend/fused_moe_decode.cu#L424) now dispatches to warp vs legacy based on `NEMOTRON_FORWARD_EXPERT_SELECT_LEGACY=1` and whether the shape is `n_group==1`, `topk_group==1`, `n_routed_experts<=128`.

2. Compile status: `cmake --build build-phase1-tests --target nemotron_runtime_backend -j4` succeeded. `cmake --build build-phase1-tests --target full_forward_manifest_smoke_test -j4` also succeeded when run sequentially. The only failure I saw was from launching both targets at the same time and racing on `runtime/libnemotron_runtime_backend.a`.

3. Concerns about correctness vs the legacy kernel: For the Nano path, the fast path is designed to match legacy selection behavior, including lower-index tie breaking and producing `-1`/`0.0f` if no finite candidate remains. Grouped routing or `>128` experts do not use the new kernel; they still fall back to legacy. I did not run a numeric diff against the legacy kernel or benchmark timings in this turn.
